{"kind":"assert","text":"CF-2 item 2, pre-code proposal. Item 1 remains a separate comments-and-documents commit on the next gated head; this act changes no source.\n\nCurrent behavior at the CF-1 source: publication-paths.ts:54-56 compares literal prefixes with startsWith; base-directory.ts:96-104 applies that comparison to creating/created applications. readPublicationPrefixes also uses it to reject overlap inside one declaration. Destination and the publication host use withinPublicationPrefixes for literal path authorization. Thus Shelf/ and shelf/ can be separate claims today.\n\nProposed claim comparison: split valid prefixes into path segments, then derive a comparison key for each segment with Unicode canonical caseless matching, NFD(toCasefold(NFD(segment))). Use full default case folding, with pinned Unicode data; do not use locale-sensitive lowercasing. This is Unicode18 section3.13 D145: https://www.unicode.org/versions/Unicode18.0.0/core-spec/chapter-3/. Original spelling remains in signed declarations, stored rows and emitted paths. The derived key is only for collision/refusal decisions.\n\nRefuse a declaration whose two distinct prefixes become equal or ancestor/descendant under those segment keys. Refuse a new application's claim with publication-prefix-in-use when its normalized segment sequence equals, contains or lies within a creating/created application's claim. Examples: Shelf/ against shelf/; Shelf/ against shelf/chapter/; composed café/ against cafe followed by U+0301/; Straße/ against STRASSE/. shelf/ and shelf-other/ remain disjoint. A creating/created claim still holds its reservation, and refused/conflicting creation still releases it. The existing path-shape, byte/count limits and reserved-path refusals remain in force.\n\nDo not widen path authorization by applying the folded compare to withinPublicationPrefixes. A declared shelf/ does not gain authority to emit Shelf/file. A publisher can keep its declared spelling. The new claim guard prevents two applications from owning aliases; it does not claim to prevent one publisher from emitting two alias filenames inside its own tree. If that wider tree property is desired, the version's request must name it separately.\n\nAllocation read: current base is Rules15, Directory27, Destination21, Register28. No Rules16 pin occurs in the checked source or design notes. H3 request18c73b6b requires a new order creation family and explicitly defers its version allocation until post-CF1 source; it has no allocated Rules number. H5's package-install refusal is also still a pending choice, O10. I propose Rules16 as the next new Rules version carrying this behavior, paired with the new Directory claim guard and whatever new creation-family pins admit it. Keep Rules15 and Directory27 byte-identical so existing family replay stays unchanged. Do not alter the shared helper in a way that changes old pins.\n\nPlease confirm whether Rules16 belongs to the H3 family request, the later H5 install request, or another pending Rules allocation before source opens. I have asked this allocation question in workroom chat rather than reserve a number independently. No deployment waits on item2, as CF-2 states. The future version needs collision witnesses for same-list and cross-application case/canonical/ancestor collisions, a disjoint-segment witness, and an old-family versus new-family pair. No tests, builds or compilers ran for this proposal."}